  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis this afternoon highlight weak \nridging over the Rockies and Plains as a potent mid/upper wave sits \njust off the CA coast. Meanwhile, a low-level moisture plume spans \nfrom the Gulf northward through the Missouri River Valley and \nbeyond. A surface high resides in IL.\n\nLater this afternoon into the evening hours, daytime heating amid a \nweakly-capped atmosphere may allow for an isolated storm or two over \nlocations along/east of the Flint Hills. Lack of forcing remains the \nprimary concern for coverage. However, considering PWs about 175% \nof normal for this time of year, decided to hold low chances in \nplace as it wouldn't be a surprise to see some development prior to \nsundown.\n\nAs we move into the overnight hours, easterly winds settling in \nacross the forecast area on east will help advect rich low-level \nmoisture into the region. This moistening along with weak upslope \nflow may allow for light drizzle and patchy fog into early Monday \nmorning across mainly the Flint Hills east and along/north of US-50. \nThere remains a bit of uncertainty as to whether this will make it \ninto south central KS at this time.\n\nQuiet weather is expected for Monday with ridging remaining in \nplace. By late Monday night/Tuesday morning, the aforementioned \npotent upper impulse will be located over the Desert Southwest. \nIncreasing low-level moisture transport ahead of this will allow for \nshowers and storms to develop over the area Tuesday morning into the \nafternoon. As the mid/upper wave approaches and pulls in the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo, coverage of showers and storms will \nincrease from the Desert Southwest into the Central Plains through \nmid/late week. Details are still coming in to focus, though at this \ntime a majority of the deterministic/ensemble suite carry the \ngreatest rainfall totals to the northwest and southeast of the \nforecast area. Still looking like the best chances for widespread \nheavy rain for us will come Wednesday night into Thursday morning as \na cold front tied to upper energy tracking across the Canadian \nPrairie slams southeast through the Central Plains. In addition to \nthis, models show the upper low cutting off over the Southern Plains \nfollowing its ejection out of the Rockies. This may keep lingering \nrain chances over southeast KS heading into next weekend.\n\nRegarding temperatures this coming week, afternoon highs ranging \nfrom the middle 70s to the low/mid 80s are forecast through \nWednesday. Toward the back half of the week, the aforementioned cold \nfront may bring cooler temperatures (highs closer to 70 and lows in \nthe 50s) to the forecast area to close out the work week.",
